          "_label": "Details (Official List Description)"
        }
      ],
      "content": "FLAMSTEAD             HIGH STREET TL 0714                                        (North side) 9/20                                   Nos. 1, 3, 5, and 7                                        (Saunders Almshouses) (Formerly listed 22.10.52                                        as Saunder's Almshouses)                                        II* GV Block of 4 almshouses. Stone plaque in middle of front engraved 'AnoDm/ 1669\".  For  Thomas Saunders  of  Beechwood.  Reconditioned  1952,  with outshut set back at rear rebuilt. Narrow dark red brick in an unusually early use of Flemish-bond. Steep old red tile roof. A long single-storey block of 2 handed pairs of almshouses facing S. 2 large shared internal chimneys symmetrically placed on ridge each with 2 conjoined lozenge shaped diagonal shafts with corbelled caps. Moulded brick gable parapets with square piers topped by ball finials at each apex and above each corbelled brick kneeler. 4 windows S front with 2 pairs of round arched chamfered brick doorways opposite the chimneys. Recessed square-headed 2-light windows with chamfered brick jambs  and mullions  (formerly plastered to represent  stone)  and  leaded glazing  in metal  frames (renewed), oak plank doors. Excellent brickwork with bull-nose plinth and brick eaves cornice with frieze. Moulded brick corbels to gable kneelers, 2 large rectangles of stone over the western pair of doors may have  had  armorial  bearings.  Stone  set  immediately  under  central datestone may have inscription now illegible. Each single-cell house was for a poor married couple. Entrance at jamb of fireplace. Beam runs from front to back. Flat-roofed continuous rear outshut in red brick. (VCH (1908)193:  RCHM  (1911)89:  Kelly  (1914)101:  Pevsner  (1977)143:  RCHM Typescript). Listing NGR: TL0783314611"
    }
  ],
